#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065191} :activity/id #uuid "47f4640b-e4b7-5133-a3a1-63ce3b9d9596" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048180 :topic/id #uuid "863badb9-fd80-557c-8deb-101ce376c451" :topic/math-academy-id 374} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065337 :tutorial/content "The **quotient rule for exponents** states that when we divide two exponents that have the same base, we take the difference of the exponents in the numerator and denominator:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a^n}{a^m}=a^{n-m}\n$$\n\nFor example, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}^{5}}{{a}^{2}} & = \\\\ {a}^{(5-2)} & = \\\\ {a}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo see why the quotient rule is true, let's take the above example and work it out again, the long way.\n\nFor the two terms $a^5$ and $a^2$ we have $a^5 = a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a$ and $a^2 = a \\cdot a.$ If we now substitute these two expressions into $\\dfrac{a^5}{a^2},$ we get the same result:\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\eqalign{\n\\dfrac{a^{5}}{a^2}&=\\\n\\dfrac {a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {a \\cdot a}&=\\\n\\dfrac {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} \\cdot a \\cdot a \\cdot a} {\\cancel{a} \\cdot \\cancel{a} }&=\\\na \\cdot a \\cdot a &=\\\n a^3\n}\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule is just a shortcut for the process above." :tutorial/id #uuid "5ce1b58b-8395-5986-99e6-cfd10bbca653" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1478 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "6663dad9-c326-5db6-92b4-d33b07b35ae2" :step/math-academy-id 3518 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065192}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186065192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064298 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186063919 :question/id #uuid "46a52c71-86ed-5e9c-a1cb-f2110a4c6484" :question/math-academy-id "e-1073"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "577ff555-4622-5388-8bc3-b49fa39c5674" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049081